WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:17.088
GPUI is the GPU-rendered Rust UI framework behind the Zed editor. It expects you to write your interface in Rust. In this tutorial, we'll read a GPUI app written entirely in Osprey, run its tests without a window, and change how it draws.

00:17.838 --> 00:29.090
This is GPUI's own painting example, ported to Osprey. Black squares, translucent paths, a gradient star, a pie chart, a wave, and freehand lines you draw with the mouse.

00:29.290 --> 00:35.406
Every shape in this window comes from Osprey code. Rust is only the host that puts it on the GPU.

00:36.156 --> 00:40.870
Here's how the work is split. GPUI does the rendering, on the GPU.

00:41.070 --> 00:46.393
The Rust host opens the window and forwards events. It holds no application logic.

00:46.593 --> 00:50.888
Osprey owns the state, handles every event, and describes every shape.

00:51.088 --> 00:57.722
And the port's README reports zero differing pixels against the original Rust example, at the same window size.

00:58.472 --> 01:03.988
Start at the boundary. The whole app is two functions the host calls through C, and one import.

01:04.188 --> 01:11.586
Start takes the saved session, and runs the framework's start with the app's initial state, its regions, its saver and its theme.

01:11.786 --> 01:19.257
Dispatch takes the saved state and one event from the host, and returns what changed. That's the entire contract with Rust.

01:20.007 --> 01:29.359
The store is plain typed data. A Sketch holds the freehand lines, each a flat list of coordinates, plus where the current drag started, and whether you're drawing.

01:29.559 --> 01:33.168
Style holds one flag, dashed. The Store is just those two.

01:33.368 --> 01:42.739
And Act names everything the interface can send: toggle dash, clear, and draw. It's a closed set, so the compiler checks every match covers all three.

01:43.489 --> 01:46.211
Initial is an empty sketch with solid strokes.

01:46.411 --> 01:53.592
Tapped handles the two buttons: toggle the dash, or clear. Every branch returns a new store. Nothing is mutated.

01:53.792 --> 02:02.455
Controlled handles the canvas. A pointer event goes to the drawing rules, with its position, whether the button is down, and whether Shift is held.

02:03.205 --> 02:08.719
Here's a rule straight from the original. Without Shift, the point you dragged to is used as it is.

02:08.919 --> 02:21.570
With Shift held, the line stays straight. If you moved more across than down, it keeps the start's y. Otherwise, it keeps the start's x. Same behaviour as the Rust version, in a few lines of pure functions.

02:22.320 --> 02:25.992
Pressed handles one pointer event. A release ends the drag.

02:26.193 --> 02:33.711
A press starts a new line at the pointer. A drag while drawing extends the last line by one point, straightened if Shift is held.

02:33.911 --> 02:38.142
And toggle dash just flips the flag. The view decides what that looks like.

02:38.892 --> 02:46.516
The view describes shapes as data. The lightning bolt is a filled polygon with four corners, in the original's exact colour.

02:46.716 --> 02:55.271
The star is ten line segments, filled with an Oklab gradient. These values were copied from the Rust example, which is why the pixels match.

02:56.021 --> 03:01.501
Dashes is where the style becomes geometry: four units on, two off, or a solid line.

03:01.701 --> 03:08.700
Freehand turns each of your lines into a path. A line with fewer than two points draws nothing, just like the original.

03:08.900 --> 03:15.390
Canvas puts it together: the fixed drawing, then every freehand line, as one list of shapes the host renders.

03:16.140 --> 03:23.911
The header's button reads Dashed when lines are solid, and Solid when they're dashed. It's computed from state, never toggled by hand.

03:24.111 --> 03:36.785
Regions tells the framework what to rebuild. When the sketch changes, only the canvas is redrawn. When the style changes, the canvas and the header are. The host gets only the regions that changed.

03:37.535 --> 03:47.130
Run the tests. They drive the real start and dispatch boundary, with no window. The first checks that start draws the original's five quads and sixteen paths.

03:47.330 --> 03:51.462
Others draw lines, and check that Shift straightens them in both directions.

03:51.662 --> 04:00.504
And the headless self-test drives the same app through the GPUI host's own self-test steps, without opening a window. Seven steps pass.

04:01.254 --> 04:05.362
Now change it. Make the dash eight on and two off, instead of four and two.

04:06.112 --> 04:11.818
The dash test fails, and tells you exactly why: it expected four and two, and got eight and two.

04:12.018 --> 04:19.193
Six pass, one fails. The rest of the app is untouched, and the tests prove it. Put it back to four, and all seven pass.

04:19.943 --> 04:31.671
There's a second way to write a GPUI app in Osprey: as one machine document. The casting player's interface is data. Each layer of state machines has exactly one current state.

04:31.871 --> 04:44.084
Transitions are named, and guarded by inputs, like the TV reporting that it's buffering. The document is checked when it loads, and the only Osprey code is a small adapter that answers its requests.

04:44.834 --> 04:58.397
What to know. These runs are the GPUI host on macOS. On Windows, the same apps run on the WinUI host. And pixel parity with the Rust original is checked with the compare script in the reference folder.

04:59.147 --> 05:09.724
To try it, run the painting tests, then open the app with one make command, and read the source. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects, and tell us what breaks.
